summary:
In recent years, the use of stereotactic radiotherapy of the epileptogenic zone has been investigated as a potential alternative to surgical intervention in patients with pharmacoresistant epilepsy. This involves precise and non-invasive external irradiation of the epileptogenic area, either in a single session or in several fractions. Before irradiation is carried out, the epileptogenic area is localized in detail in close cooperation between the radiotherapy, neuroradiology, epileptology and neurosurgery departments. A review from 2018 identified a total of 170 patients in the international literature who were treated with radiotherapy in this context (Eekers et al., 2018). Across all studies, a reduction in seizure frequency (defined as the absence or very rare occurrence of seizures) was observed in 58% of patients (range 25-95%) within two years of radiotherapy.

The aim of this study is to carry out targeted irradiation of the epileptogenic zone in patients with pharmacoresistant epilepsy for whom surgery is not an option or is declined. The primary aim is to test the effectiveness (i.e. the change in seizure frequency) of a homogeneous and radiobiologically supported treatment concept.The use of radiotherapy in this context is therefore to be regarded as experimental. The study is single-arm and mainly serves to generate new hypotheses.The results and tolerability of the therapy are recorded prospectively, structured and longitudinally by means of a standardized follow-up, which takes into account not only clinical results but also the subjective assessments and evaluations of the patients.  This is intended to improve knowledge regarding radiotherapy for pharmacoresistant epilepsy for which surgery is not an option or is declined. In addition, the role of radiotherapy should be defined more precisely in the future.

The following data and factors will be collected:
- Patient characteristics: biometric data, type and course of epilepsy, type and course and frequency and triggers of seizures, anti-epileptic medication and dosage, localization and extent of epileptogenic zone, secondary diseases, social factors, results of clinical neurological examination, neuropsychological functional diagnostics, results of technical diagnostic examinations (laboratory diagnostics where available, cMRI, [video] EEG);
- Intervention characteristics: localization and extent of the target volume, proximity to organs at risk (OARs), dosimetric parameters, dose and fractionation of radiation;
- acute and chronic side effects of radiotherapy (relevant CTCAE items v4.03, LENT-SOM[A]);
- Results up to 10 years after completion of therapy through structured follow-up (6 weeks after completion of radiotherapy, every 3 months in the first two years, then every six months until five years, then annually until 10 years): change in seizure frequency (defined using the radiotherapy-adapted Engel classification [RAEC]), days per month without seizures, need for adjustment of antiepileptic drugs, results of clinical neurological examination, imaging follow-up checks using cMRI (first check after 6 months, from then on annually in order to be able to recognize possible late effects of radiation), subjective assessment of quality of life (EQ-5D-5L, EORTC QLQ-C30/BN20+, QOLIE-10), patient-reported experiences and decision regret (Ottawa scale), results of EEGs (if applicable video EEGs) and neuropsychological functional diagnostics. The technical examinations are carried out as part of the usual radiotherapeutic and epileptological follow-up care.

criteria:
Inclusion criteria:
- Epilepsy diagnosis (of the structural non-neoplastic type) made by an epileptologist and considered pharmacoresistant and inoperable (e.g. due to localization in an eloquent zone or an unacceptable perioperative risk due to underlying or concomitant diseases) or surgery is refused by the patient;
- Imaging evidence by means of cMRI of an anatomical region that correlates with the epilepsy diagnosis and can be clearly defined (this is done by means of an interdisciplinary conference where radiotherapists, neuroradiologists, neurosurgeons and epileptologists are present);
- multidisciplinary review of the indication for radiotherapy by radiotherapy and epileptology, with the patient being classified as a suitable candidate for stereotactic radiosurgery;
- written informed consent.
Exclusion criteria:
- pregnancy;
- history of radiotherapy in the central nervous system;
- patients with cancer predisposition syndromes (e.g. Li-Fraumeni) where each radiation treatment must be indicated with extreme caution.
